  "worked_solution": "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5sin\u2061(2x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin\u2061(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{5sin\u2061(2x)}=\\frac{1}{5}csc\u2061(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/e3f5c484211ef0b9cf5f3a29198d25c23933a1c07427d0dcbfad01edbb300d0e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{5}csc\u2061(2x)$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5}csc\u2061(2x)=\\infty .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
